0001e|module actlow
0002e|
0003e|TITLE   'Functional example of active low output signals, 22 July 1992
0004e|         John Gromala, Data I/O Corporation.'
0005e|
0006e|" Generic example file demonstrating how active low signals are treated
0007e|" in simulation (PLASIM).
0008e|
0009e|actlow DEVICE 'p22v10';
0010e|
0011e|"inputs
0012e|
0013e|      clock  pin 1;
0014e|      in1    pin 2;
0015e|      in2    pin 3;
0016e|
0017e|"outputs
0018e|
0019e|      !out1   pin 23 ISTYPE 'reg,buffer'; "active low signal
0020e|       out2   pin 22 ISTYPE 'reg,buffer'; "active high signal
0021e|
0022e|      !out3   pin 21 ISTYPE 'reg,invert'; "active low signal
0023e|       out4   pin 20 ISTYPE 'reg,invert'; "active high signal
0024e|
0025e|" Constant assignments
0026e|
0027e|      x, c, H, L  =  .x., .c., 1, 0 ;
0028e|
0029e|" Set Declaration
0030e|
0031e|      outputs  =  [ out1, out2, out3, out4 ];
0032e|
0033e|equations
0034e|
0035e|      out1   :=   in1  &  in2 ;
0036e|      out2   :=   in1  &  in2 ;
0037e|
0038e|      out3   :=   in1  &  in2 ;
0039e|      out4   :=   in1  &  in2 ;
0040e|
0041e|      outputs.clk  =  clock ;
0042e|
0043e|test_vectors
0044e|
0045e|      ([ clock, in1, in2 ] -> [ out1, out2, out3, out4 ])
0046e|
0047e|"The first vector is for the power up state.  The registers all 
0048e|"power up LOW but the values at the pins are based on whether there
0049e|"is an inverter or buffer from the register to the pin.  Since out3
0050e|"and out4 have inverters they will be HIGH at their pins as 
0051e|"demonstrated by the test vectors (look at simulator results).
0052e|
0053e|       [   0  ,  x ,  x  ] -> [  x  ,  x  ,  x  ,  x   ]; 
0054e| 
0055e|"The second vector is written for the equations in the equation section.  
0056e|"These are all active high equations and the test vectors should be written
0057e|"to test the logic for these equations.  However, when you view the simulator 
0058e|"results, signals one and three will be HIGH for their values even though 
0059e|"the vector is written with LOWS.  The reason being these are actually
0060e|"active low signals and when the logic is not true their values should be HIGH.
0061e|
0062e|       [   c  ,  0 ,  0  ] -> [  L  ,  L  ,  L  ,  L   ];
0063e|
0064e|" This vector demonstrates the same as the previous vector except for
0065e|"now the logic is true and the signals, one and three, have LOWS as
0066e|"their values in the simulator results.  However, you write the vector
0067e|"for active high logic and thus the highs in the actual vector written.
0068e|
0069e|       [   c  ,  1 ,  1  ] -> [  H  ,  H  ,  H  ,  H   ];
0070e|       [   c  ,  0 ,  1  ] -> [  L  ,  L  ,  L  ,  L   ];
0071e|
0072e|end
0073e|
0074e|
0075e|
